Hi all,

Suppose I have:

myList <- list(a=c("aa", "bb"), b=c("ee", "ffg", "gg", "dd"))

I want to find:
1. Which vector contains "ffg"?
2. Which vector contains string that is substring of "Alaaaaa"?

Thanks,

Avner

From u9d7c257e609d0807 at groups.google.com  Thu Jul 24 12:23:06 2014
From: u9d7c257e609d0807 at groups.google.com (amit gal)
Date: Thu, 24 Jul 2014 12:23:06 +0000
Subject: Re: [Israel RUG] Text processing
Message-ID: <nFseSi7vDDE.KcP_X3j0mUMJ@israel-r-user-group.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <nFseSi7vDDE.RRXGYYL3eCIJ@israel-r-user-group.groups.google.com>
References: <nFseSi7vDDE.RRXGYYL3eCIJ@israel-r-user-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/israel-r-user-group/c/nFseSi7vDDE/m/KcP_X3j0mUMJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

1) sapply(myList, function(x) "ffg" %in% x)
2) sapply(myList,function(t) {a = sum(sapply(t,grepl,x="Alaaa")); a>0})

number 2 is really awkward and I'm sure there is some text/string analysis packages that has some more user friendly functionality (which will probably also solve 1)

